And then probably a quick demo.

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:11.920
And then wrap up with Q&amp;A.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:14.650
So the first phase
of the journey

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:19.660
from building applications from
monoliths to microservices.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:25.180
Consider building a simple image
rendering ML application where

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:27.880
you have a web app running
a couple of modules,

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:31.060
such as a rendering module
and a retrieval module,

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:33.380
and a user comes in and,
say, uploads an image, say,

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:35.530
for example, a cat image.

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:39.900
And then you run some rendering
work, process that image,

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:42.070
detect that it's a cat,
frame it, and store it

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:43.720
in the back-end.

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:47.030
And then retrieve the image,
and then say, hey, I'm a cat,

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:49.220
it's detected and so on.

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:51.640
If you build this on a
traditional web server

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:55.000
with a bunch of
modules, we have what we

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.320
call a monolithic application.

00:01:57.320 --> 00:01:58.870
While building
these applications,

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:01.510
we may end up with
certain problems.

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:03.880
And the challenges with
such an application,

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:06.630
or limitations in
such an approach,

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:09.880
is that we may have
scalability issues.

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:12.170
It's difficult to scale
independent components,

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:14.500
such as the rendering module
or the retrieval module

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:16.930
independently.

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:19.830
It's hard to scale these modules
on independent dimensions,

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:22.800
such as, for example,
just CPU or memory.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:26.040
Further, you have slower
developer velocity.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:27.490
What it may face
here is that you

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:30.220
can't do continuous
deployment, you

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:33.350
have to redeploy your
entire application,

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:35.670
furthermore, you have
a large monolithic code

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:38.740
base, which is
difficult to maintain,

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:42.040
and it prevents teams from
working independently.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:44.500
Additionally, you're
also committing yourself

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:47.750
to a long term technology stack.

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:50.500
Now, if you were to rewrite
the same application using

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:54.010
microservices, where you
had the same image rendering

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:56.620
application, but you had
two independent services,

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:59.950
a rendering service and
a retrieval service,

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:03.580
and the user comes in, uploads
a cat image, it goes back,

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:07.520
it's rendered, stored in
the back-end, and retrieved.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.925
Now, each service
operates independently.

00:03:10.925 --> 00:03:13.300
And there are certain benefits
of building an application

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.490
in this fashion.

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:19.600
The first is that you
have better scalability.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:24.310
You can isolate hot services
and scale them independently.

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:27.160
Services can be scaled on
independent dimensions,

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:29.800
such as CPU and memory.

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:33.940
Further, you have faster
developer velocity.

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:36.990
It supports
continuous deployment.

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:40.960
Your code base can be organized
by each independent service.

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:44.950
Thereby, allowing you to
isolate these services

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:46.940
for testing and deployment.

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:48.670
And additionally, it
enables your teams

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:52.330
to work independently
and be more productive.

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:55.090
Lastly, it has a
shorter term commitment

00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:57.610
to the same technology stack.

00:03:57.610 --> 00:04:00.490
So building an application
in such a microservices

00:04:00.490 --> 00:04:04.850
architecture, you may have
certain additional needs.

00:04:04.850 --> 00:04:07.750
One of the key needs
is having a task queue.

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:10.520
So why do you need a task queue?

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:13.150
For example, if you go back
to the same image rendering

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:15.400
application, and suppose
your rendering service

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:20.589
was rendering 100 images,
and say, in a single loop.

00:04:20.589 --> 00:04:22.960
Imagine if there is a failure
in even one of the images,

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:25.630
then you have
chances of failure,

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:28.960
your application
could break and so on.

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:31.460
This could lead to
higher user latency.

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:33.160
Further, you have
lesser guarantees

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:36.160
of making sure your images
get actually rendered.

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:37.810
And a single point
of failure can

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:40.260
break your entire application.

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:44.830
Now, if you were to just change
this and fork off the rendering

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:50.470
tasks to a queue and make the
queue process to work for you

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:54.340
to minimize user latency
and isolate failures

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:56.500
of, say, a single image,
and also, give you

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:59.970
a guarantee of at
least one execution,

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:02.200
you're making sure that
your application gives

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:04.930
the right level of service to
your users with the right level

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:07.060
of reliability guarantees.

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:08.830
Think of this as
like just the way

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:11.260
you would fork off threads
while writing your application

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:14.170
in any programming language,
and now you're just doing that

00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:16.290
as threads for microservices.

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:19.120
So that is the motivation
for having a task queue when

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:22.410
you write up microservices.

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:24.630
So now, if you take
this a step further,

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:27.390
and you had many services,
like a user authentication

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:29.940
service along with the rendering
service and image upload

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:32.820
service, each of
these services could

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:36.000
upload independent tasks,
or asynchronous tasks,

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:37.110
to your queue.

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:40.020
One of them can say,
hey, when the user

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:42.040
is logging in for
the first time,

00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:44.250
kick off certain
deferred emails that can

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:46.867
be sent a month later for
registration purposes.

00:05:46.867 --> 00:05:48.450
You can have your
image rendering task

00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:51.270
submitted as well to
the same task queue,

00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:52.980
and each of these
services can then

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:56.250
consume the end results of
these tasks accordingly.

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:57.960
By doing this, this
task queue let's

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:01.470
these services interact
in a more different way,

00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:04.410
and, thereby, let you write up
these applications for greater

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:07.680
scalability, as we
had talked of before.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:10.470
So given this
context, I would like

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:14.160
to now invite Bart,
who's a customer trying

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:15.990
to build an image
rendering application,

00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:19.670
and he wants to check if Google
can help him in using a task

00:06:19.670 --> 00:06:22.393
queue in the process.

00:06:22.393 --> 00:06:24.680
BART HAZER: If I'm
a developer breaking

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:26.980
