00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:58.930
for companies that sell digital
solutions for local businesses.

00:07:58.930 --> 00:08:01.880
So for the purposes
of this demo,

00:08:01.880 --> 00:08:03.360
you can just really think of--

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:05.500
we build products
for local businesses,

00:08:05.500 --> 00:08:10.710
whether that's restaurants
or doctors or maybe

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:14.280
car dealerships, just
a local business.

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:17.490
And so we've been
around for 10 years now.

00:08:17.490 --> 00:08:20.050
And when we started
as a company,

00:08:20.050 --> 00:08:22.530
it was really about the
time that App Engine

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:24.070
was released by Google.

00:08:24.070 --> 00:08:27.970
And so ever since then, we've
been an App Engine customer,

00:08:27.970 --> 00:08:30.000
but even just more
as a GCP customer.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:34.223
And we've really utilized
all the products.

00:08:34.223 --> 00:08:38.390
We probably use them all,
maybe except for Genomics.

00:08:38.390 --> 00:08:41.820
And in kind of the
last couple of years,

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:45.630
we've been migrating from
this App Engine, Python, Cloud

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:46.340
Datastore.

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:48.900
And we probably have
15 of these projects.

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:53.940
And we've now decided to embrace
Kubernetes Engine, Go lang,

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:55.200
and Cloud Spanner.

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:59.070
And so over the
last two years we've

00:08:59.070 --> 00:09:01.902
deployed and shipped
75 of these services.

00:09:01.902 --> 00:09:03.360
And so something
that's really core

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:06.930
to the identity of our
company is that we really

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:09.610
rely on Google to offer
us managed solutions,

00:09:09.610 --> 00:09:14.790
so whether that's for databases
or infrastructure, everything.

00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:16.150
We don't have our own servers.

00:09:16.150 --> 00:09:17.920
We've never had our own servers.

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:22.570
We've always relied on the
cloud for our business.

00:09:22.570 --> 00:09:26.910
And so Cloud Tasks has been an
important part of App Engine.

00:09:26.910 --> 00:09:30.690
And just until recently,
we didn't this ability

00:09:30.690 --> 00:09:33.870
to use Cloud Tasks, until just--

00:09:33.870 --> 00:09:36.240
as Vinod is releasing
an alpha for being

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:38.278
able to push to non
App Engine targets,

00:09:38.278 --> 00:09:39.570
which was very exciting for us.

00:09:39.570 --> 00:09:42.710
It's been a missing
tool kit for us.

00:09:42.710 --> 00:09:48.330
And so the history with our task
queues is, being an App Engine,

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:49.740
if you're not
familiar with that,

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:53.880
is that everything
always gets put in

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:56.160
through as an HTTP request.

00:09:56.160 --> 00:09:59.920
You can't spin off background
requests or background work.

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:01.920
Everything's always coming
on that HTTP request.

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:04.268
And for these requests,
you have a 10 minute limit.

00:10:04.268 --> 00:10:06.060
And so when you're
thinking of all the work

00:10:06.060 --> 00:10:08.750
that you might need to do for,
maybe you're generating billing

00:10:08.750 --> 00:10:12.510
reports, or any of
these complex workflows,

00:10:12.510 --> 00:10:15.930
you have to think about how
you can divide that work

00:10:15.930 --> 00:10:19.930
and actually finish it
within a 10 minute window.

00:10:19.930 --> 00:10:21.750
And so it really
forces people to think

00:10:21.750 --> 00:10:25.980
about separating these
pieces out and running them

00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:27.240
with Cloud Tasks.

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:29.760
And so this is really no
different than running

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:31.890
applications on
Kubernetes Engine.

00:10:31.890 --> 00:10:35.820
For us, we're shipping code
to these services dozens

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:38.010
of times a day across many--

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:39.120
countless of them.

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:41.310
And so as pods are
spinning up and down,

00:10:41.310 --> 00:10:45.130
if you have long lived work,
that just doesn't really work.

00:10:45.130 --> 00:10:47.820
And so you have to have a
way to check in your states,

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:51.360
or gracefully handle
restarts, and as pods

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:52.470
are spinning up and down.

00:10:52.470 --> 00:10:54.928
There's just countless ways
that pods can come up and down.

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:02.730
And so for us, we've really
always leveraged frameworks

00:11:02.730 --> 00:11:04.690
in order to do this.

00:11:04.690 --> 00:11:06.690
The very first one that
we wrote was Fantasm.

00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:08.490
And it's a way to
run finite state

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:12.360
machines on Python and App
Engine with Cloud Tasks.

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:15.270
And then Google
released a framework

00:11:15.270 --> 00:11:18.960
called Pipelines, which is
also for Python, App Engine.

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:22.230
But instead of it being a finite
state machine, it's more a DAG

00:11:22.230 --> 00:11:23.220
based--

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:24.473
maybe like Apache Airflow--

00:11:24.473 --> 00:11:25.890
but also with
components for doing

00:11:25.890 --> 00:11:29.200
that produced kind
of applications.

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:30.960
And then just recently
we've begun work

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:34.920
on a new framework for running
Go lang Kubernetes Cloud Tasks

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:35.430
frameworks.

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:38.070
And we're going to be opening
source to that very shortly as

00:11:38.070 --> 00:11:40.518
well.

00:11:40.518 --> 00:11:42.810
So some of the things that
we've learned over the years

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:46.770
is that task queue
is a, at least,

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:48.690
[INAUDIBLE] for execution.

00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:52.320
And so we might see over
like a billion tasks

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:54.670
that you might have maybe
a couple of duplicates

00:11:54.670 --> 00:11:55.170
that happen.

00:11:55.170 --> 00:11:57.570
And so when these
things happen, you

00:11:57.570 --> 00:12:01.170
have to make sure that your
application is idempotent.

00:12:01.170 --> 00:12:03.450
Otherwise, you're going
to have very strange bugs,

00:12:03.450 --> 00:12:08.820
and it'll be very, very hard
for you to track those down.

00:12:08.820 --> 00:12:11.502
And so typically, these things--

00:12:11.502 --> 00:12:13.335
you're very cognizant
when you're writing up

00:12:13.335 --> 00:12:15.625
these applications,
and it's more over time

00:12:15.625 --> 00:12:17.250
as things are changing
in the code base

00:12:17.250 --> 00:12:20.880
and people are changing and
adding that these things become

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:21.630
not idempotent.

00:12:21.630 --> 00:12:24.450
So it's very important to
just always remember that.

00:12:24.450 --> 00:12:26.670
And then task naming
is another component

00:12:26.670 --> 00:12:31.110
that we've really leveraged for
Cloud Tasks-- is that you can

00:12:31.110 --> 00:12:35.190
give each task a
name, and that allows

00:12:35.190 --> 00:12:37.570
you to prevent duplicates
from being emitted,

00:12:37.570 --> 00:12:40.180
but it also just allows
for observability

00:12:40.180 --> 00:12:41.940
into your application.

00:12:41.940 --> 00:12:45.000
Maybe when tasks are failing
and they're retrying,

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:47.460
it's really nice to be able
to see that there's maybe

00:12:47.460 --> 00:12:49.740
a customer identifier, or
just additional metadata

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:54.240
in that name, so that you can
just more quickly figure out

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:56.980
what's actually going on.

00:12:56.980 --> 00:12:57.480
Oh, sorry.

00:12:57.480 --> 00:12:59.440
Wrong way.

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:01.170
So we have a product--

00:13:01.170 --> 00:13:02.880
it's called
Reputation Management.

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:06.288
And so for a local business--
maybe I'm a restaurant--

00:13:06.288 --> 00:13:07.830
we have customers
that are leaving us

00:13:07.830 --> 00:13:11.070
reviews across various sources.

00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:15.390
And maybe it's through
social media or blog posts,

00:13:15.390 --> 00:13:19.140
or maybe just someone would
leave a review on a website.

00:13:19.140 --> 00:13:21.150
And so we go and
collect this data

00:13:21.150 --> 00:13:23.760
and present it to an end user.

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:25.757
And we have millions
of SMBs in our system,

00:13:25.757 --> 00:13:27.840
and we have thousands of
these downstream sources.

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:29.215
And this turns
into a lot of work

00:13:29.215 --> 00:13:31.420
that we have to do every day.

00:13:31.420 --> 00:13:34.223
And so some of these problems is
that we have maybe API limits,

00:13:34.223 --> 00:13:35.640
and we have different
integrations

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:36.690
with different partners.

00:13:36.690 --> 00:13:38.490
And we need to make
sure that we're

00:13:38.490 --> 00:13:41.790
staying under these
limits, but also we

00:13:41.790 --> 00:13:45.150
just want to be a good citizen
and not unintentionally DDoS.

00:13:45.150 --> 00:13:47.280
Us being on App Engine
and Kubernetes Engine,

00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:49.050
it's very easy
for us to scale up

00:13:49.050 --> 00:13:52.410
to hundreds and thousands
of instances and nodes.

00:13:52.410 --> 00:13:54.060
Not all of these
downstream sites

00:13:54.060 --> 00:13:56.350
can handle that
amount of traffic.

00:13:56.350 --> 00:13:59.160
And so we end up trying to put
things into their own queues,

00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:01.350
but it just becomes very
challenging in having

00:14:01.350 --> 00:14:04.140
so many queues and trying
to manage them all.

00:14:04.140 --> 00:14:06.810
And so we ended up building
a system called a scheduler.

00:14:06.810 --> 00:14:10.080
And really what it does
is it allows the developer

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:12.420
to go and write a
query in BigQuery,

00:14:12.420 --> 00:14:16.650
and all of the emitting rows
that come out of that query end

00:14:16.650 --> 00:14:18.917
up getting scheduled
into Cloud Tasks.

00:14:18.917 --> 00:14:21.000
And so one of the features
that Cloud Tasks offers

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:24.270
is that you can put an
execution time of when

00:14:24.270 --> 00:14:26.080
you want that task to run.

00:14:26.080 --> 00:14:29.160
And so what this allows
us to do is run this query

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:32.005
and put in a bunch of
different heuristics

00:14:32.005 --> 00:14:33.630
on maybe how you want
to schedule them.

00:14:33.630 --> 00:14:36.780
And so when we're
looking at segmenting by,

00:14:36.780 --> 00:14:39.450
maybe the source
information, we can perfectly

00:14:39.450 --> 00:14:42.780
schedule and uniformly across
this highly dimensional data,

00:14:42.780 --> 00:14:44.620
across the entire day.

00:14:44.620 --> 00:14:46.650
And so we'll end up
having a table that looks

00:14:46.650 --> 00:14:48.450
like this, where we have--

00:14:48.450 --> 00:14:52.770
this is a table in BigQuery
where we have three businesses,

00:14:52.770 --> 00:14:56.010
and then they all want to go
out to the same source to get,

00:14:56.010 --> 00:14:58.830
maybe, the reviews.

00:14:58.830 --> 00:15:01.650
And so this is maybe a
very simplified version

00:15:01.650 --> 00:15:04.920
of that query, but,
really, all it's doing

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:07.210
is we're using the BigQuery
windowing functions,

00:15:07.210 --> 00:15:08.850
and we can partition
by the source,

00:15:08.850 --> 00:15:13.560
and we can do a bunch of date
math to smear all of the work

00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:15.520
that we need to do
across the entire day.

00:15:15.520 --> 00:15:18.510
And so this turns out to be
a very uniform amount of work

00:15:18.510 --> 00:15:20.580
that we can schedule.

00:15:20.580 --> 00:15:22.650
And so you can see here
now that at the end

00:15:22.650 --> 00:15:24.930
the day we have a
schedule time, and then we

00:15:24.930 --> 00:15:27.030
can emit that into
a task, and then

00:15:27.030 --> 00:15:28.540
those could run in the future.

00:15:28.540 --> 00:15:31.800
And so it worked out
extremely well for us.

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:34.718
And so this is a graph
here in Stackdriver that--

00:15:34.718 --> 00:15:36.510
one of the heuristics
that we've added here

00:15:36.510 --> 00:15:38.850
is that, instead of
starting all over the work

00:15:38.850 --> 00:15:43.570
at the beginning of the day,
we've, I guess, scattered it,

00:15:43.570 --> 00:15:47.080
so that we slowly start
off the amount of work.

00:15:47.080 --> 00:15:50.280
So instead of going from 0
to 100 really, really fast,

00:15:50.280 --> 00:15:53.610
we'll emit maybe 20% of it to
start, and then another 20%

00:15:53.610 --> 00:15:54.960
to start 5 minutes later.

00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:58.080
And so this allows us, our
systems, if they're cold,

00:15:58.080 --> 00:16:01.340
they can more
naturally scale up.

00:16:01.340 --> 00:16:03.540
And so just in
summary, Cloud Tasks

00:16:03.540 --> 00:16:06.000
has been pretty
much vital for us

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:08.240
in being able to build
application on App Engine

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:09.270
and in Kubernetes.

00:16:09.270 --> 00:16:11.730
And with our scheduling
system, there's

00:16:11.730 --> 00:16:15.240
a bunch of different categories
in heuristics that we can do.

00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:18.030
We can very optimize
our API usage.

00:16:18.030 --> 00:16:21.200
We can do things like adjusting
for cost and our speed.

00:16:21.200 --> 00:16:24.600
And we can even group certain
tasks together that might have

00:16:24.600 --> 00:16:27.570
a [INAUDIBLE] locality in that
you're just adding an order

00:16:27.570 --> 00:16:28.140
by--

00:16:28.140 --> 00:16:30.420
onto a column, we can
force certain things

00:16:30.420 --> 00:16:32.772
to run next to each other.

00:16:32.772 --> 00:16:34.980
And so with that, I'm going
to hand it back to Vinod.

00:16:34.980 --> 00:16:37.222
Thank you.

00:16:37.222 --> 00:16:38.127
[APPLAUSE]

00:16:38.127 --> 00:16:39.710
VINOD RAMACHANDRAN:
Thank you, Braden,

00:16:39.710 --> 00:16:42.920
for the broad overview of
your architecture and your use

00:16:42.920 --> 00:16:45.170
cases.

00:16:45.170 --> 00:16:46.950
Moving on.

00:16:46.950 --> 00:16:49.460
I would like to now do a
more deep dive of Cloud Tasks

00:16:49.460 --> 00:16:50.690
and its key salient features.

00:16:53.550 --> 00:16:57.680
Specifically, I'm going to
talk about Cloud Tasks being

00:16:57.680 --> 00:17:00.800
a point to point queuing
system for asynchronous tasks

00:17:00.800 --> 00:17:03.080
across microservices.

00:17:03.080 --> 00:17:05.329
To be more specific,
what it provides

00:17:05.329 --> 00:17:07.280
you is reliable task offloading.

00:17:07.280 --> 00:17:09.859
You can dispatch your
long running tasks.

00:17:09.859 --> 00:17:12.589
It's fully managed,
completely serverless and pay

00:17:12.589 --> 00:17:15.319
for what you use.

00:17:15.319 --> 00:17:18.589
Furthermore, it provides your
important rate limit and retry

00:17:18.589 --> 00:17:20.010
controls.

00:17:20.010 --> 00:17:21.920
This is very important
in queuing systems

00:17:21.920 --> 00:17:26.210
when you have multiple services
one after the other and tasks

00:17:26.210 --> 00:17:29.390
need to be dispatched
through those services.

00:17:29.390 --> 00:17:32.360
These rates can be
configured and customized

00:17:32.360 --> 00:17:34.490
based on your needs
for your system.

00:17:34.490 --> 00:17:38.360
I'll elaborate more on that
as we go through this talk.

00:17:38.360 --> 00:17:40.010
Furthermore, it
provides flexible

00:17:40.010 --> 00:17:44.610
routing with HTTP and HTTPS,
as well as an authentication.

00:17:44.610 --> 00:17:47.745
So you can reach any endpoint
within Google Cloud, as well as

00:17:47.745 --> 00:17:48.620
your on-prem systems.

00:17:51.170 --> 00:17:55.070
So going back to our image
rendering application.

00:17:55.070 --> 00:17:57.200
You can now fork off
your image rendering task

00:17:57.200 --> 00:17:59.210
to Cloud Tasks in Google Cloud.

00:17:59.210 --> 00:18:01.520
And you can do it
reliably, fully managed,

00:18:01.520 --> 00:18:02.630
and pay for what you use.

00:18:05.550 --> 00:18:09.100
And you can access Cloud Tasks
using various client libraries

00:18:09.100 --> 00:18:13.120
across a broad range of
programming languages.

00:18:13.120 --> 00:18:16.730
You can also use a Rest API.

00:18:16.730 --> 00:18:19.240
Further, you can configure
and monitor the queues

00:18:19.240 --> 00:18:22.783
using Intuitive CLI commands.

00:18:22.783 --> 00:18:24.700
And we have a really
good monitoring dashboard

00:18:24.700 --> 00:18:26.920
that helps you track the
progress of your tasks

00:18:26.920 --> 00:18:29.590
in your queue and take
corrective action whenever

00:18:29.590 --> 00:18:32.860
necessary.

00:18:32.860 --> 00:18:35.980
So now, diving deeper into some
of the features of the product

00:18:35.980 --> 00:18:38.430
itself.

00:18:38.430 --> 00:18:40.750
Scheduled task delivery
is an important feature

00:18:40.750 --> 00:18:42.670
that Cloud Tasks provides.

00:18:42.670 --> 00:18:46.310
Imagine you're a company that
has a subscription service

00:18:46.310 --> 00:18:49.090
and a user signs up at the start
of the month for a free trial

00:18:49.090 --> 00:18:50.140
period.

00:18:50.140 --> 00:18:54.110
And at the end of 30 days you
would like to probably send out

00:18:54.110 --> 00:18:58.630
a reminder email encouraging the
user to move from the free tier

00:18:58.630 --> 00:18:59.950
to the paid tier.

00:18:59.950 --> 00:19:02.960
Then when the user signs up,
you can schedule those tasks

00:19:02.960 --> 00:19:06.670
30 days in advance to be
triggered by Cloud Tasks,

00:19:06.670 --> 00:19:08.410
and you can trigger
a cloud function

00:19:08.410 --> 00:19:11.830
to send that email out
at the end of 30 days,

00:19:11.830 --> 00:19:14.620
and remind the user
to maybe try out

00:19:14.620 --> 00:19:16.977
the paid version of
the product and so on.

00:19:16.977 --> 00:19:19.310
Many of our customers use the
product for this use case.

00:19:19.310 --> 00:19:22.980
And hence, I wanted
to highlight it out.

00:19:22.980 --> 00:19:24.432
The next feature
is rate controls.

00:19:24.432 --> 00:19:26.140
And I'm going to spend
a couple of slides

00:19:26.140 --> 00:19:28.660
on this as this is important.

00:19:28.660 --> 00:19:31.000
So like imagine Cloud
Tasks or any queuing system

00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:32.500
dispatching tasks
at a certain rate,

00:19:32.500 --> 00:19:35.120
for example, 10 tasks
per second and so on.

00:19:35.120 --> 00:19:37.120
You can do it at
that particular rate,

00:19:37.120 --> 00:19:39.190
or you could set
configurable parameters

00:19:39.190 --> 00:19:42.960
and then cut down the
rate to maybe a lower rate

00:19:42.960 --> 00:19:44.990
and dispatch it at that rate.

00:19:44.990 --> 00:19:47.320
Why is this important?

00:19:47.320 --> 00:19:49.330
For example, in
queuing systems, assume

00:19:49.330 --> 00:19:51.340
that you have a bunch
of services stacked one

00:19:51.340 --> 00:19:54.460
after the other and each one
has a different processing

00:19:54.460 --> 00:19:55.540
capacity.

00:19:55.540 --> 00:19:57.160
So, for example,
here service one

00:19:57.160 --> 00:19:59.440
has a capacity of
10 tasks per second,

00:19:59.440 --> 00:20:02.773
and service two has a capacity
of five tasks per second.

00:20:02.773 --> 00:20:04.190
But if you start
dispatching tasks

00:20:04.190 --> 00:20:07.120
at, say, a much higher
rate, then the weakest link

00:20:07.120 --> 00:20:10.780
in the chain, then you're going
to reach a state of a system

00:20:10.780 --> 00:20:12.250
not being in equilibrium.

00:20:12.250 --> 00:20:14.110
You're going to see
a lot of failures.

00:20:14.110 --> 00:20:16.443
And then you'll have to take
a lot of corrective actions

00:20:16.443 --> 00:20:17.560
to fix that issue.

00:20:17.560 --> 00:20:21.550
By having a queuing system that
can help you dispatch tasks

00:20:21.550 --> 00:20:25.690
at a configurable rate, the
most optimal link in the chain,

00:20:25.690 --> 00:20:28.562
you'll be able to set up
a system for equilibrium.

00:20:28.562 --> 00:20:30.520
And the entire flaw in
the system, for example,

00:20:30.520 --> 00:20:32.472
here it's five tasks per second.

00:20:32.472 --> 00:20:34.930
For many of you who work in
supply chain systems and so on,

00:20:34.930 --> 00:20:36.100
this is very common.

00:20:36.100 --> 00:20:38.147
It's very much like
inventory management--

00:20:38.147 --> 00:20:39.730
making sure that
each hop in the chain

00:20:39.730 --> 00:20:41.440
has the right level
of inventory and it's

00:20:41.440 --> 00:20:43.720
processing the throughput
at the right rate.

00:20:43.720 --> 00:20:47.020
And this is standard queuing
system practice, but having--

00:20:47.020 --> 00:20:50.290
controlling that rate from the
source of the entire system

00:20:50.290 --> 00:20:51.420
is very powerful.

00:20:51.420 --> 00:20:55.390
And hence, I wanted
to call it out here.

00:20:55.390 --> 00:20:59.050
Further, moving on-- having
good retry semantics.

00:20:59.050 --> 00:21:02.260
So going back to
the email use case.

00:21:02.260 --> 00:21:04.690
At the end of 30 days, if I
were a subscription company,

00:21:04.690 --> 00:21:07.870
I really want to make sure that
that email reaches my users.

00:21:07.870 --> 00:21:09.370
So I want to make
sure I have really

00:21:09.370 --> 00:21:10.720
powerful retry semantics.

00:21:10.720 --> 00:21:12.400
I want it to retry many times.

00:21:12.400 --> 00:21:13.890
Not only that,
during failures, I

00:21:13.890 --> 00:21:16.810
want to make sure that the
time gap between the retries

00:21:16.810 --> 00:21:17.850
is minimal.

00:21:17.850 --> 00:21:20.390
I also retry for a
certain period of time.

00:21:20.390 --> 00:21:23.800
So I can set really intuitive
parameters in Cloud Tasks

00:21:23.800 --> 00:21:26.920
to make you achieve that goal.

00:21:26.920 --> 00:21:30.370
Similarly, you may have certain
tasks or long running processes

00:21:30.370 --> 00:21:34.120
that may not be as
user latency specific

00:21:34.120 --> 00:21:35.650
and may be delay tolerant.

00:21:35.650 --> 00:21:38.320
For example, if you're
running ML model training

00:21:38.320 --> 00:21:42.040
workloads that
dispatch in the night

00:21:42.040 --> 00:21:45.130
and then the model is stored
eventually in the cloud,

00:21:45.130 --> 00:21:47.710
you want those things to
run, but you don't want--

00:21:47.710 --> 00:21:49.840
you may have more
delay tolerance,

00:21:49.840 --> 00:21:52.630
so you can set lesser
number of retries,

00:21:52.630 --> 00:21:55.500
maybe the intervals switching
the retries are longer,

00:21:55.500 --> 00:21:58.170
and you may retry over
a longer duration.

00:21:58.170 --> 00:21:59.800
And by having
configurable parameters

00:21:59.800 --> 00:22:01.353
to help you achieve
those goals, it's

00:22:01.353 --> 00:22:03.020
something that Cloud
Tasks provides you.

00:22:06.290 --> 00:22:09.100
Furthermore and lastly,
flexible routing is something

00:22:09.100 --> 00:22:11.620
that I've spoken about before.

00:22:11.620 --> 00:22:14.870
So Cloud Tasks lets you
reach multiple endpoints

00:22:14.870 --> 00:22:19.630
with HTTP, HTTPS, as well as
with OAuth and authentication,

00:22:19.630 --> 00:22:23.888
across different services in a
cloud, such as Cloud Functions.

00:22:23.888 --> 00:22:25.930
If you have a microservice
running on Kubernetes,

00:22:25.930 --> 00:22:27.250
you can reach that.

00:22:27.250 --> 00:22:30.310
Further, we've launched
new serverless offerings,

00:22:30.310 --> 00:22:33.070
which is Cloud Run and Cloud
Run GKE, in this conference.

00:22:33.070 --> 00:22:34.778
If you have your
service running on that,

00:22:34.778 --> 00:22:38.020
you can reach there, as well
as your on-prem systems.

00:22:38.020 --> 00:22:40.210
By letting you reach these
broad ranges of services

00:22:40.210 --> 00:22:42.390
across the cloud and
on prem, Cloud Tasks

00:22:42.390 --> 00:22:44.380
let's you enqueue
tasks from anywhere

00:22:44.380 --> 00:22:46.932
and reach these services.

00:22:46.932 --> 00:22:48.640
So with that, I would
like to now move on

00:22:48.640 --> 00:22:52.130
to a demo, demonstrating
some of these features,

00:22:52.130 --> 00:22:54.280
and then we can move to Q&amp;A.

00:22:54.280 --> 00:22:57.080
So could we move
to the demo slides?

00:22:57.080 --> 00:22:57.580
Perfect.

00:22:57.580 --> 00:22:58.590
Thank you.

00:22:58.590 --> 00:23:02.680
So I wanted to give you a
brief overview of the demo

00:23:02.680 --> 00:23:06.040
architecture before I start
demonstrating key use cases.

00:23:06.040 --> 00:23:09.370
So what I have here is the same
image rendering application

00:23:09.370 --> 00:23:12.470
where in step one a
user submits an image,

00:23:12.470 --> 00:23:15.970
these images get
enqueued in Cloud Tasks,

00:23:15.970 --> 00:23:18.430
and then they hit an
image rendering service,

00:23:18.430 --> 00:23:20.590
which is running on Cloud Run.

00:23:20.590 --> 00:23:22.450
Cloud Run is a
service offering which

00:23:22.450 --> 00:23:23.678
we launched in Cloud Next.

00:23:23.678 --> 00:23:25.220
And the reason I
pick Cloud Run is it

00:23:25.220 --> 00:23:27.770
easily scales up and
scales down to zero.

00:23:27.770 --> 00:23:29.930
So if you don't have
any requests coming in,

00:23:29.930 --> 00:23:32.980
it'll scale down to zero,
and it's truly serverless.

00:23:32.980 --> 00:23:35.110
And this particular
image rendering service

00:23:35.110 --> 00:23:36.730
pulls up images
from Cloud Storage,

00:23:36.730 --> 00:23:40.105
and step three sends
those-- sends the data

00:23:40.105 --> 00:23:42.910
to an object rendering
microservice running

00:23:42.910 --> 00:23:45.340
on Cloud Run on GKE.

00:23:45.340 --> 00:23:47.390
And Cloud Run on GKE is
the Cloud Run experience,

00:23:47.390 --> 00:23:48.550
but it's on GKE.

00:23:48.550 --> 00:23:50.200
And the reason
I've chosen that is

00:23:50.200 --> 00:23:52.660
because I want to do
this processing on a GPU,

00:23:52.660 --> 00:23:54.430
because I want to
do it in seconds,

00:23:54.430 --> 00:23:55.880
and GKE lets you do that.

00:23:55.880 --> 00:23:57.910
You can configure
a GPU node pool

00:23:57.910 --> 00:23:59.330
and do very quick processing.

00:23:59.330 --> 00:24:00.937
That's the benefit of the cloud.

00:24:00.937 --> 00:24:02.770
And the end result is
then eventually stored

