00:28:02.450 --> 00:28:04.780
So the next step that
I'm going to do is--

00:28:04.780 --> 00:28:06.920
in queuing systems,
you always have

00:28:06.920 --> 00:28:09.620
something as what you call
a poison pill message,

00:28:09.620 --> 00:28:11.360
or a poison pill task.

00:28:11.360 --> 00:28:13.650
So it's a task that's
always going to fail.

00:28:13.650 --> 00:28:15.440
And I know many of
you, while debugging,

00:28:15.440 --> 00:28:18.320
have always tried to find
the needle from the haystack.

00:28:18.320 --> 00:28:20.150
And here, what I'm
trying to do is

00:28:20.150 --> 00:28:21.920
I'm going to submit
an invalid image,

00:28:21.920 --> 00:28:24.710
and this task is
going to always fail.

00:28:24.710 --> 00:28:26.930
And I'm going to demonstrate
how task keeps retrying.

00:28:26.930 --> 00:28:29.840
And I'm going to demonstrate
that particular failure use

00:28:29.840 --> 00:28:31.380
case.

00:28:31.380 --> 00:28:33.300
So I'm going to do
that same thing,

00:28:33.300 --> 00:28:38.980
but I'm going to submit this
particular invalid image.

00:28:38.980 --> 00:28:40.690
The task got created.

00:28:40.690 --> 00:28:44.110
And I come to the UI and I
see, OK, already the cloud task

00:28:44.110 --> 00:28:45.260
has retried several times.

00:28:45.260 --> 00:28:46.760
I've set a very low
retry frequency.

00:28:46.760 --> 00:28:50.720
It's 0.1 seconds, so it's
going to keep on trying.

00:28:50.720 --> 00:28:52.340
I'm going to say run now.

00:28:52.340 --> 00:28:54.330
It's going to fail again.

00:28:54.330 --> 00:28:55.550
And this is really useful.

00:28:55.550 --> 00:28:58.070
I know root cause analysis
is a key thing that people

00:28:58.070 --> 00:29:00.320
look for in queuing
systems, and Cloud Tasks

00:29:00.320 --> 00:29:01.370
helps you achieve that.

00:29:01.370 --> 00:29:03.020
All these operations that
I've done from the UI,

00:29:03.020 --> 00:29:05.270
you can do from the CLI, as
well as the client library.

00:29:05.270 --> 00:29:06.710
If you have thousands
of tasks, it

00:29:06.710 --> 00:29:10.370
doesn't make sense to the user
UI to do this kind of analysis.

00:29:10.370 --> 00:29:12.260
But I just wanted to
demonstrate the power

00:29:12.260 --> 00:29:14.520
of this particular product.

00:29:14.520 --> 00:29:17.060
So moving on, I'm going
to, again, delete this task

00:29:17.060 --> 00:29:19.880
from this queue, and then
