00:37:19.780 --> 00:37:21.772
Is that higher rate
really much better?

00:37:21.772 --> 00:37:23.730
Can I do like five times
this particular value?

00:37:23.730 --> 00:37:26.200
Can I set it to 500
per second and so on?

00:37:26.200 --> 00:37:28.770
So the only way to do that is
you experiment with that value,

00:37:28.770 --> 00:37:30.603
and then you try to see
if there are errors,

00:37:30.603 --> 00:37:33.090
and then you kind of figure
out your optimal sweet spot

00:37:33.090 --> 00:37:35.680
with that particular experiment.

00:37:35.680 --> 00:37:38.400
So I'm going to now set the
same thing to 500 per second.

00:37:43.090 --> 00:37:46.532
So if we come back here
and set it to 500 second,

00:37:46.532 --> 00:37:48.115
there are a lot of
tasks in the queue,

00:37:48.115 --> 00:37:52.050
and there are a lot of-- the
tasks are getting processed.

00:37:52.050 --> 00:37:54.550
But you can certainly see that
there's been a sudden change.

00:37:54.550 --> 00:37:56.830
You see some errors popping up.

00:37:56.830 --> 00:37:58.480
You see some kind of failures.

00:37:58.480 --> 00:38:01.180
The rate at which it was
processing at 100 versus 500

00:38:01.180 --> 00:38:03.560
seems to be different.

00:38:03.560 --> 00:38:07.000
And this basically can
be illustrated further

00:38:07.000 --> 00:38:10.060
by looking at a
list of the tasks.

00:38:10.060 --> 00:38:11.860
You can do that through the CLI.

00:38:11.860 --> 00:38:13.435
So I'm going to do
a list of the CLI.

00:38:17.910 --> 00:38:20.560
I'm going to list all
the tasks from the CLI.

00:38:20.560 --> 00:38:23.150
And then we've got to see
if there are any errors.

00:38:23.150 --> 00:38:26.440
So let's just do that.

00:38:32.553 --> 00:38:33.970
So what I've done
is I've just run

00:38:33.970 --> 00:38:35.770
a command which
lists all the tasks

00:38:35.770 --> 00:38:37.210
in the queue at this point.

00:38:37.210 --> 00:38:40.210
And you can see at this point
where we have a lot of errors

00:38:40.210 --> 00:38:43.190
all over the place,
these internal errors.

00:38:43.190 --> 00:38:45.340
So I set a much higher
rate for the queue,

00:38:45.340 --> 00:38:48.970
and I figure out, oh, OK, this
is probably not the right rate.

00:38:48.970 --> 00:38:51.375
So basically now I
need to step back

00:38:51.375 --> 00:38:52.750
and then set it
to a value, which

00:38:52.750 --> 00:38:58.640
is basically what I thought
was an earlier optimal value.

00:38:58.640 --> 00:39:00.670
And the reason I was
able to replicate this

00:39:00.670 --> 00:39:05.290
is because I had
forcefully set the end

00:39:05.290 --> 00:39:07.280
cluster to not scale further.

00:39:07.280 --> 00:39:10.910
So I had reached the capacity
of that particular system.

00:39:10.910 --> 00:39:12.880
And then my sensory
analysis helped

00:39:12.880 --> 00:39:15.958
me find out the optimal rate
for this particular queue.

00:39:15.958 --> 00:39:18.000
And that's pretty much
what helped me figure out,

00:39:18.000 --> 00:39:21.220
OK, I can do 100 QPS
and I should be fine.

00:39:21.220 --> 00:39:23.170
If later on something
comes up where

00:39:23.170 --> 00:39:25.690
I need to reduce it further,
increase it further,

00:39:25.690 --> 00:39:30.125
I can configure it and take
corrective action as necessary.

00:39:30.125 --> 00:39:31.000
So that was the demo.

00:39:31.000 --> 00:39:32.792
Could we switch back
to the slides, please?

00:39:41.420 --> 00:39:42.620
Great.

00:39:42.620 --> 00:39:45.200
So just a quick recap as well.

00:39:45.200 --> 00:39:47.120
So when I created
that rate limiting

00:39:47.120 --> 00:39:50.615
problem, and the place
where I had created the--

00:39:50.615 --> 00:39:54.010
I disabled auto scaling was the
object detection microservice.

00:39:54.010 --> 00:39:56.510
If you have any other service
where you have such rate limit

00:39:56.510 --> 00:39:58.880
concerns, like if
you're hitting an API,

00:39:58.880 --> 00:40:01.333
or where there are
API limits and so on,

00:40:01.333 --> 00:40:03.500
you probably need to have
some kind of rate limiting

00:40:03.500 --> 00:40:06.170
from your source to make sure
you don't reach those APIs

00:40:06.170 --> 00:40:09.830
limits for cost saving
purposes, or even,

00:40:09.830 --> 00:40:12.200
in this case,
scalability purposes.

00:40:12.200 --> 00:40:14.758
The corrective action I took
was I reset the rate limit.

00:40:14.758 --> 00:40:16.800
But you could actually do
something else as well.

00:40:16.800 --> 00:40:19.880
You could double the
capacity at your end service.

00:40:19.880 --> 00:40:21.800
You could double
your GPU node pool.

00:40:21.800 --> 00:40:24.350
You could do vertical
scaling and move from a GPU

00:40:24.350 --> 00:40:25.820
to a higher GPU.

00:40:25.820 --> 00:40:28.460
And you can then drain all
the tasks from your queue.

00:40:28.460 --> 00:40:31.282
It all depends on
the user's needs.

00:40:31.282 --> 00:40:32.740
Like, if there are
two personas who

00:40:32.740 --> 00:40:34.157
are using this
particular product,

00:40:34.157 --> 00:40:38.420
one is a user who is enqueueing
tasks from an application

00:40:38.420 --> 00:40:40.640
perspective, and a sysadmin
persona who's actually

00:40:40.640 --> 00:40:42.860
monitoring the
queue, the sysadmin

00:40:42.860 --> 00:40:44.390
can take corrective
action depending

00:40:44.390 --> 00:40:45.620
on what needs to be done.

00:40:45.620 --> 00:40:48.560
He can increase capacity, or
he could limit rate depending

00:40:48.560 --> 00:40:51.080
on the need of the [INAUDIBLE].

00:40:51.080 --> 00:40:54.140
So moving on, I
wanted to summarize.

00:40:54.140 --> 00:40:55.970
Cloud Tasks is a
point to point queuing

00:40:55.970 --> 00:40:58.100
system for asynchronous tasks.

00:40:58.100 --> 00:41:00.650
It offers great features,
such as rate limits,

00:41:00.650 --> 00:41:03.110
retry controls,
dynamic scheduling,

00:41:03.110 --> 00:41:05.750
dynamic HTTP targets
across different services

00:41:05.750 --> 00:41:08.070
in the cloud, as well
as on-prem systems,

00:41:08.070 --> 00:41:10.160
and it provides your
HTTP with authentication.

00:41:16.460 --> 00:41:19.810
[MUSIC PLAYING]

